  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nScattered showers, more numerous north of Pittsburgh, are\nexpected this morning, initially in warm advection along a\nsurface boundary across northern Ohio into NW PA. Mesoanalysis\nand water vapor satellite imagery currently shows a vort max\nacross the NE Indiana area. This should track ESE across the\nregion through this morning. Additional scattered showers are\nexpected to develop and track across the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion this morning as it crosses. \n\nAnother vort max is progged to cross the Lower Great Lakes\nregion this afternoon, with scattered showers/storms north of\nPIT, where the best lift and moisture is progged. becoming more\nisolated to the south. This activity should wane by late\nafternoon as the wave exits.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA ridge is expected to build eastward from the Central CONUS\nthrough mid week. The highest 500mb heights are progged to be\nacross the Upper Ohio Valley region on Wednesday. As the ridge\nbuilds eastward, shortwaves are expected to track along the\nnorthern/northeastern periphery of the ridge, crossing the lower\nGreat Lakes/Upper Ohio Valley region. Occasional shower and\nthunderstorm chances are expected as these waves cross, one\ntonight (mainly north of the forecast area,) with another\npossible later Tuesday. There remains uncertainty in the\nplacement and strength of the ridge, and the resulting track of\nany shortwaves on the northern periphery. \n\nThere is a conditional potential for severe storms on Tuesday.\nML CAPE increases to between 2000 and 3000 j/kg by late\nafternoon, with veering in the low levels. An initial warm \nlayer aloft could limit convection, though a possible shortwave \npassage in the afternoon could trigger thunderstorms. This \nscenario remains uncertain due to the reasons mentioned above, \nthough if storms are able to develop severe weather will be \npossible. \n\nThe day with the lowest potential for showers and thunderstorms\nappears to be Wednesday, when the ridge is progged to be the\nstrongest across the Upper Ohio Valley region. Current\nindications are the ridge should begin to break down late\nWednesday and Thursday, as a shortwave and surface cold front \napproaching. The potential for severe storms will also increase\nby Wednesday evening, increasing again on Thursday as convection\nincreases in diurnal heating along the slow moving front.\nIncreasing NW flow aloft should result in increasing shear, as\nwell as increasing diurnal instability. The potential continues\nfor severe storms into Thursday. The latest ML guidance\nindicates the increasing severe potential, especially late\nWednesday and Thursday. Depending on how fast the front drifts\nsouth, this potential could continue for at least southern\nportions of the area into Friday. Dry weather should return by\nthe weekend as the front exits and high pressure builds in.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nRising 500mb heights with the building ridge should result in\nwarmer temperatures each day through mid week, with the heat\npeaking Wednesday when the ridge is strongest across the Upper\nOhio Valley region. Most locations outside of the higher terrain\nshould reach the 85 to 90 degree range on Tuesday, though there\nis a continued potential for convection and/or patchy cloud\ncover which could affect daytime highs. The heat should peak\nWednesday, as highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s for most \nareas. The combination of heat and humidity should result in \nheat index values in the mid to upper 90s Tuesday and Wednesday.",
